{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfcSm2LkygtC8zb9V3kEm", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "To cut a release, push a git tag named `vYYYY.MM.N`, for example `v2026.10.1`. CI then builds and publishes it. Don't publish anything by hand.\n\n```sh\ngit checkout main && git pull\ngit tag v2026.10.1\ngit push origin v2026.10.1\n```\n\n`N` is the release number within that month, so the first release in October 2026 is `v2026.10.1`.\n\nThis comes from a project note saved in the team's shared memory, not from anything in the repo. When I checked the repo, three things didn't fully back it up:\n\n- **I couldn't find the CI setup.** There's no `.github/workflows/` or other CI config here, so whatever builds and publishes on a tag lives somewhere else. I can't confirm from the repo that pushing a tag actually triggers a publish.\n- **There are no existing tags.** I couldn't check the naming against past releases.\n- **The version in `pyproject.toml:3` is written by hand.** It's `version = \"2026.9.1\"`. Nothing I found says whether to update it to match the tag before tagging. If the build reads its version from that file, a tag-only release would go out labelled `2026.9.1`. Check with whoever owns the CI pipeline.\n\nIf you confirm how the version should be handled, I can add it to the project note."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 376, "cache_read_input_tokens": 25647, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 376}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "0e19faba-8331-4be0-85ba-a52b2215fa06", "uuid": "219f5370-f262-4098-8c14-b7f0a7f6b40e", "timestamp": "2026-10-02T00:14:05.8